23:02:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The Earth’s temperature hasn’t “always stayed the same”—it has varied over geological time (ice ages, warm periods, etc.). However, it remains relatively stable over long periods because of an **energy balance**: the Earth constantly radiates heat back into space. - **Incoming energy** from the Sun (mostly visible light) warms the planet. - **Outgoing energy** is emitted as infrared radiation, escaping to space. The Earth’s average temperature stabilizes where the incoming and outgoing energy flows match. If the Earth heated up without bound, it would emit more infrared (hotter objects radiate more), eventually reaching a new equilibrium. The greenhouse effect temporarily traps some of that outgoing heat, but the balance still holds over time. So the Sun adds heat, but Earth loses heat at the same rate, preventing a runaway temperature increase. Changes in this balance (due to greenhouse gases, solar variations, ice cover, etc.) do cause climate shifts, but the system always tends toward a steady state.
